  "title": "Anolis richardii",
  "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anolis_richardii",
  "revision": 1354952849,
  "sections": [
   {
    "h": null,
    "level": 1,
    "paras": [
     "Anolis richardii, commonly known as the Grenada tree anole or Richard's anole, is a species of anole lizard in the family Dactyloidae. The species is found in the Caribbean."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Etymology",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "The specific name, richardii, is in honor of French botanist Louis Claude Marie Richard."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Geographic range",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "A. richardii is native to Grenada and the Grenadines islands, and it has been introduced to Tobago."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Description",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "A. richardii is large for an anole, with males reaching a maximum length of 140 mm (5.5 in) snout-to-vent.  It has a dark green or brown dorsal surface, with a green-gray to yellow ventral surface.  Its dewlap is orange, yellow, or gray-green.  Females and juveniles often have a yellow or cream-colored lateral stripe."
    ]
   }
  ]
 }
